<GuidePage
  slug="how-to-form-an-llc-in-washington"
  title="How to Form an LLC in Washington State (Step by Step)"
  seoTitle="How to Form an LLC in Washington: 6 Steps (2026 Guide)"
  description="Form a Washington LLC in 6 steps: pick a name, choose a registered agent, file with the Secretary of State, get an EIN and apply for your business license."
  eyebrow="Washington LLC guide"
  intro="Forming an LLC in Washington takes six steps across two state agencies and the IRS. Here is the whole process in plain language, in the order you should do it."
  updated="November 2026"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Washington splits LLC setup between the <strong>Secretary of State</strong>, which creates your company, and the <strong>Department of Revenue</strong>, which licenses it to do business. Missing the second half is the most common mistake we see. Here is everything in order.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1: Choose your LLC name</h2>
  <p>
    Your name must be different from every other business already registered with the Washington Secretary of State, and it must end with <strong>"Limited Liability Company," "LLC" or "L.L.C."</strong> Search the Secretary of State's Corporations and Charities Filing System (CCFS) before you settle on one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Plan to use your name without "LLC" on signs, trucks and invoices? You'll also want a trade name. See <a href="/washington-trade-name-dba">our guide to Washington trade names</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 2: Name a registered agent</h2>
  <p>
    Every Washington LLC must have a registered agent: a person or company with a physical Washington street address (not a P.O. box) who accepts legal papers and state notices for your business during normal business hours. You can serve as your own agent, but your address becomes public record.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 3: File your Certificate of Formation</h2>
  <p>
    The Certificate of Formation is the document that legally creates your LLC. You file it with the Secretary of State, online through CCFS or by mail. Online filing costs <strong>$200</strong> ($180 filing fee plus a $20 online processing fee).
  </p>
  <p>
    File your <strong>Initial Report</strong> at the same time. It lists your principal office and governors, and it's free when filed with the Certificate of Formation (it costs $10 if you file it later).
  </p>

  <h2>Step 4: Get your EIN from the IRS</h2>
  <p>
    An Employer Identification Number is your business's federal tax ID. You need it to open a business bank account, hire employees and file federal taxes. Getting one directly from the IRS is free. Be wary of websites that charge for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 5: Apply for your Washington business license</h2>
  <p>
    This is the step people miss. File a <strong>Business License Application</strong> with the Department of Revenue's Business Licensing Service. It registers you for Washington taxes, including B&amp;O tax, and is where you add:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Trade names</strong>, $5 each</li>
    <li><strong>City endorsements</strong>, if your city requires its own business license</li>
    <li><strong>State endorsements</strong>, for industries that need special licensing</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Fees vary with what your business needs. See <a href="/washington-llc-cost">what a Washington LLC costs</a> for the full breakdown.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 6: Set up your new company</h2>
  <p>Once the state paperwork is done, a few things keep your LLC protected and running smoothly:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Write an operating agreement.</strong> Washington doesn't require you to file one, but you should have one.</li>
    <li><strong>Open a business bank account.</strong> Keeping business and personal money separate is a big part of what protects you.</li>
    <li><strong>Set up bookkeeping.</strong> Washington taxes gross receipts, not profit, so your books need to track revenue the way the state sees it. <a href="https://washingtonbookkeeper.com">Washington small business bookkeeping</a> can help.</li>
    <li><strong>Mark your annual report date.</strong> It's due every year by the end of the month your LLC was formed.</li>
    <li><strong>File your excise tax returns</strong> on the schedule the Department of Revenue assigns you, even in months with no income.</li>
  </ul>

